1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,340
Melty and magnificent, creamy and dreamy,

2
00:00:04,440 --> 00:00:07,700
whether it's mozzarella, manchego, American, or Swiss,

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:10,740
when you take a snapshot of America's favorite foods,

4
00:00:10,840 --> 00:00:12,540
you got to say cheese.

5
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:14,440
Last year, the average American

6
00:00:14,540 --> 00:00:17,960
ate a whopping 40 pounds of cheese.

7
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:20,160
That's like taking two of the tires on your car,

8
00:00:20,260 --> 00:00:23,460
turning them into cheese, and then eating the tires.

9
00:00:23,560 --> 00:00:28,300
Last year, over 95% of American households bought cheese,

10
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:32,300
which makes the cheese business bring in a whole lot of cheddar.

11
00:00:32,301 --> 00:00:37,300
Annual sales last year topped $34 billion.

12
00:00:37,301 --> 00:00:41,300
Annual production is around 13 billion pounds.

13
00:00:41,301 --> 00:00:43,300
That's almost equivalent to the weight

14
00:00:43,301 --> 00:00:46,300
of a million African elephants.

15
00:00:46,301 --> 00:00:49,300
Cheese makers around the country are working super hard

16
00:00:49,301 --> 00:00:53,300
to keep up with the demand, and I want to know how they do it.

17
00:00:53,301 --> 00:00:56,300
Just like fondue, we're going to immerse ourselves

18
00:00:56,301 --> 00:00:58,800
into all things cheese.

19
00:00:58,801 --> 00:01:00,600
I just want to go double-dutch with this

20
00:01:00,601 --> 00:01:01,800
for the rest of my life.

21
00:01:01,801 --> 00:01:05,300
From how it's made to where the most expensive cheese

22
00:01:05,301 --> 00:01:09,640
comes from, we'll learn how much mac and cheese we eat a year,

23
00:01:09,641 --> 00:01:14,560
the origins of string cheese, and why it peels the way it does.

24
00:01:14,561 --> 00:01:17,860
We'll even find out where Philadelphia cream cheese

25
00:01:17,861 --> 00:01:19,560
really comes from.

26
00:01:19,561 --> 00:01:23,460
And we've been granted access to a dark underground cave

27
00:01:23,461 --> 00:01:25,500
in the last place you'd ever expect it

28
00:01:25,501 --> 00:01:31,000
to find out how cheese is aged to moldy magnificence.

29
00:01:31,001 --> 00:01:35,380
This is Modern Marvel's The United States of Cheese.

30
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:51,080
Today, I'm at Caputo Brothers Creamery,

31
00:01:51,081 --> 00:01:53,460
an artisan cheesemaker who has a great

32
00:01:53,461 --> 00:01:55,500
has been praised by Food & Wine magazine

33
00:01:55,501 --> 00:01:58,940
and some of the most prestigious restaurants in New York

34
00:01:58,941 --> 00:02:02,340
for their Italian-style mozzarella and ricotta.

35
00:02:02,341 --> 00:02:06,040
Caputo Brothers gets milk from local farms and turns it,

36
00:02:06,041 --> 00:02:08,100
or maybe I should say churns it,

37
00:02:08,101 --> 00:02:12,740
into 6,700 pounds of cheese every week.

38
00:02:12,741 --> 00:02:16,640
That's 350,000 pounds of cheese per year.

39
00:02:16,641 --> 00:02:18,680
And though they deliver all this cheese

40
00:02:18,681 --> 00:02:20,960
to each of the 50 states, they still

41
00:02:20,961 --> 00:02:23,960
to their traditional Italian techniques.

42
00:02:23,961 --> 00:02:25,960
And most of that is done by hand.

43
00:02:25,961 --> 00:02:28,960
So I figure that this is the perfect place

44
00:02:28,961 --> 00:02:32,960
for some real deal, step-by-step, hands-on instruction

45
00:02:32,961 --> 00:02:35,960
in how cheese is made.

46
00:02:35,961 --> 00:02:36,960
I'm excited to get started.

47
00:02:36,961 --> 00:02:37,960
I'm getting hungry.

48
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:43,960
Dave and Rin Caputo started Caputo Brothers Creamery

49
00:02:43,961 --> 00:02:46,960
as a small cheese baking operation in their home

50
00:02:46,961 --> 00:02:49,960
and grew into one of the premier artisan cheese marines.

51
00:02:49,961 --> 00:02:53,300
artisan cheese-making businesses in the country.

52
00:02:53,301 --> 00:02:56,200
The ricotta salata took home a blue ribbon

53
00:02:56,201 --> 00:02:59,360
for the best traditional cheese produced in America

54
00:02:59,361 --> 00:03:02,540
and went toe-to-toe with their Italian counterparts

55
00:03:02,541 --> 00:03:05,240
in the World Cheese Awards.

56
00:03:05,241 --> 00:03:07,640
I'm so excited to try this because the truth of the matter

57
00:03:07,641 --> 00:03:10,880
is cheese is not all created equal.

58
00:03:10,881 --> 00:03:14,160
Would you be willing to impart just a fraction

59
00:03:14,161 --> 00:03:18,760
of your cheese knowledge to one very, very earnest student?

60
00:03:18,761 --> 00:03:20,260
Will you be my cheese sensei?

61
00:03:20,261 --> 00:03:21,260
I think so.

62
00:03:21,261 --> 00:03:22,260
I think so.

63
00:03:22,261 --> 00:03:24,100
I would be your provolone padawan.

64
00:03:24,101 --> 00:03:25,260
Excellent.

65
00:03:25,261 --> 00:03:27,100
So if I want to learn the process, where do I begin?

66
00:03:27,101 --> 00:03:29,940
Well, we know that all good cheese starts with good milk.

67
00:03:29,941 --> 00:03:30,940
Of course.

68
00:03:30,941 --> 00:03:31,940
So you got to start there first.

69
00:03:31,941 --> 00:03:32,940
So head on out that way.

70
00:03:32,941 --> 00:03:34,240
You'll get the milk in the building,

71
00:03:34,241 --> 00:03:35,780
and then we'll get you all suited up to make cheese.

72
00:03:35,781 --> 00:03:39,940
OK, let's check out this milk and get the process moving.

73
00:03:39,941 --> 00:03:41,780
Please don't change the channel because of that joke.

74
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:52,760
110 billion gallons of milk every year.

75
00:03:52,761 --> 00:03:55,260
And some of the best milk on the planet

76
00:03:55,261 --> 00:03:58,260
comes from the pastures of central Pennsylvania.

77
00:03:58,261 --> 00:04:01,180
Caputo Brothers gets all its milk from dairies

78
00:04:01,181 --> 00:04:03,940
within 30 miles of their location.

79
00:04:03,941 --> 00:04:07,980
I'm meeting with Mike, the so-called herd-to-curred manager

80
00:04:07,981 --> 00:04:09,820
who oversees this process.

81
00:04:09,821 --> 00:04:12,520
This massive dairy locomotive that we're looking at here,

82
00:04:12,521 --> 00:04:13,660
how much milk does it hold?

83
00:04:13,661 --> 00:04:17,960
This trailer holds roughly 63,000 pounds, 7,000 gallons.

84
00:04:17,961 --> 00:04:20,000
This milk will go into all products?

85
00:04:20,001 --> 00:04:23,440
Or like, do you do this farm we only use for the mozzarella

86
00:04:23,441 --> 00:04:25,940
and the stracciatelle, this one we only use for the ricotta?

87
00:04:25,941 --> 00:04:31,240
We do a blended ratio of our dairy farms together.

88
00:04:31,241 --> 00:04:34,280
So each farm has a different flavor characteristic?

89
00:04:34,281 --> 00:04:35,220
Absolutely.

90
00:04:35,221 --> 00:04:35,940
Is that right?

91
00:04:35,941 --> 00:04:36,940
Absolutely.

92
00:04:36,941 --> 00:04:39,240
So farm A may be known for sweeter milks,

93
00:04:39,241 --> 00:04:42,220
farm B may be known for more grassy milks or something

94
00:04:42,221 --> 00:04:43,220
like that?

95
00:04:43,221 --> 00:04:45,660
And this particular milk is what kind of cow

96
00:04:45,661 --> 00:04:46,720
are we getting today?

97
00:04:46,721 --> 00:04:47,920
It's a blended...

98
00:04:47,921 --> 00:04:49,020
Oh, this has already been blended.

99
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:49,980
Yep, yep.

100
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:52,540
So he stops at each of the farms.

101
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:53,340
Is that right?

102
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:55,420
And brings all of it at one time.

103
00:04:55,460 --> 00:04:56,240
I love that.

104
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:58,340
The place that's manufacturing the cheese

105
00:04:58,341 --> 00:05:02,100
is getting a custom blend from within a half hour of its front door.

106
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:06,280
That kind of quality control is something that you very, very seldom find.

107
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:11,000
Although there are thousands of different variations

108
00:05:11,001 --> 00:05:12,580
in the cheese-making process,

109
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,480
resulting in thousands of different types of cheese,

110
00:05:15,481 --> 00:05:17,980
there are essentially four basic steps

111
00:05:17,981 --> 00:05:20,980
to convert milk into cheese.

112
00:05:20,981 --> 00:05:24,480
The first step to making cheese is adding bacteria.

113
00:05:24,481 --> 00:05:26,820
And this bacteria lowers the pH of the milk,

114
00:05:26,821 --> 00:05:29,720
making it more acidic, which prevents bad bacteria

115
00:05:29,721 --> 00:05:33,040
that can make you sick from growing on the cheese later on.

116
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:36,840
The second step is adding enzymes to coagulate the milk.

117
00:05:36,841 --> 00:05:40,140
Traditionally, these enzymes come in the form of rennet.

118
00:05:40,141 --> 00:05:42,540
What rennet does is it coagulates the milk,

119
00:05:42,541 --> 00:05:45,080
causing the solid curds to come together

120
00:05:45,081 --> 00:05:47,420
and separate from the liquid whey.

121
00:05:47,421 --> 00:05:49,080
And you almost have cheese,

122
00:05:49,081 --> 00:05:52,040
you just have to age it or ripen it.

123
00:05:52,041 --> 00:05:53,880
The conditions under which you age it

124
00:05:53,881 --> 00:05:56,380
are the thing that determines what type of cheese

125
00:05:56,381 --> 00:05:57,880
you end up with.

126
00:05:57,881 --> 00:06:00,920
Cheeses range from soft to hard,

127
00:06:00,921 --> 00:06:02,420
stinky to salty,

128
00:06:02,421 --> 00:06:04,160
but many experts would group them

129
00:06:04,161 --> 00:06:07,160
into one of seven main categories.

130
00:06:07,161 --> 00:06:09,740
Number one, soft cheese, like brie.

131
00:06:09,741 --> 00:06:14,520
Number two, semi-soft cheese, like Monterey Jack or Munster.

132
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:18,580
Number three is hard cheeses, like cheddar, manchego,

133
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:19,680
or Parmesan.

134
00:06:19,780 --> 00:06:23,520
Coming in at number four, the good old stinky blue cheeses.

135
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:26,360
Number five is cheese with mix-ins,

136
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:30,360
like cheddar with jalapeno or gouda with truffles mixed in.

137
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:34,560
Category six is fresh cheese, like the mozzarella and ricotta

138
00:06:34,660 --> 00:06:36,060
we're making at Caputo.

139
00:06:36,061 --> 00:06:39,760
And finally, there are aged fresh cheeses.

140
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,200
Now that we have the milk in the building,

141
00:06:45,480 --> 00:06:48,140
Elizabeth, the head cheesemaker for Caputo Brothers,

142
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:53,160
is going to show us how to make their traditional Italian fresh mozzarella.

143
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:55,820
But before we do anything to the milk,

144
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:59,720
I want to taste the raw stuff straight from the cow.

145
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:01,760
Is it safe to taste, Romo?

146
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,140
It is safe, and it's legal here in Pennsylvania.

147
00:07:04,141 --> 00:07:07,040
I've eaten all over the world and eaten all kinds of wonderfuls.

148
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:08,640
I have never had raw milk.

149
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:11,240
This is the first raw milk I am consuming.

150
00:07:11,340 --> 00:07:12,540
This is a food first.

151
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:13,780
I am very, very excited.

152
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:21,500
This is pure, frothy raw milk, straight from the cow to me.

153
00:07:21,600 --> 00:07:23,000
I'm like nervous and excited.

154
00:07:23,001 --> 00:07:23,840
I'm excited.

155
00:07:33,340 --> 00:07:36,740
I mean, clean is like the top note that I want to get.

156
00:07:36,741 --> 00:07:40,240
I mean, it's nice and cold, and maybe on the very back end,

157
00:07:40,241 --> 00:07:44,020
there's something like a grassier kind of note to it.

158
00:07:44,021 --> 00:07:46,760
It has a greater degree of thickness to it.

159
00:07:46,761 --> 00:07:49,960
Yeah, we often describe raw milk as more like a milkshake

160
00:07:49,961 --> 00:07:51,200
in consistency.

161
00:07:51,201 --> 00:07:52,660
That's pretty accurate.

162
00:07:52,661 --> 00:07:54,660
And because of the blend you use,

163
00:07:54,661 --> 00:07:56,660
it's really unlike any milk I've had,

164
00:07:56,661 --> 00:07:59,660
because most commercial dairies just have one kind of cow,

165
00:07:59,661 --> 00:08:02,660
because they're not trying to blend it into cheese.

166
00:08:02,661 --> 00:08:05,660
It's really, really good, though.

167
00:08:05,661 --> 00:08:09,660
Now we're ready to get down to making this mozzarella.

168
00:08:09,661 --> 00:08:13,660
At Caputo's, they start with pasteurizing the milk,

169
00:08:13,661 --> 00:08:16,660
a process of basically heating it to a temperature

170
00:08:16,661 --> 00:08:18,660
for a sustained amount of time

171
00:08:18,661 --> 00:08:21,660
to kill off any pathogens and bad bacteria.

172
00:08:21,661 --> 00:08:25,000
So we're up to temperature, and it's ready to send out.

173
00:08:25,001 --> 00:08:27,040
Now that we have pasteurized the milk,

174
00:08:27,041 --> 00:08:30,660
the real process of cheese making begins.

175
00:08:30,661 --> 00:08:33,800
We're looking at a giant vat of pasteurized milk,

176
00:08:33,801 --> 00:08:37,900
meaning most of the bacteria, good and bad, has been removed.

177
00:08:37,901 --> 00:08:41,720
And since most cheese making requires microbial magic,

178
00:08:41,721 --> 00:08:45,480
we need to add some bacteria cultures back in.

179
00:08:45,481 --> 00:08:47,620
So the next step is adding culture.

180
00:08:47,621 --> 00:08:48,480
OK.

181
00:08:48,481 --> 00:08:50,420
We use a lactobacillus culture.

182
00:08:50,421 --> 00:08:52,380
Can you adjust that little bit for all this milk?

183
00:08:52,381 --> 00:08:55,660
It does not take very much to do what we need to do with it.

184
00:08:55,661 --> 00:08:57,920
So it's bacteria in powdered form?

185
00:08:57,921 --> 00:08:59,840
It is in a freeze-dried form.

186
00:08:59,841 --> 00:09:02,780
We're just going to sprinkle the top of the milk with it.

187
00:09:02,781 --> 00:09:05,360
OK, let's do it.

188
00:09:05,361 --> 00:09:06,880
And like over there, is this good?

189
00:09:06,881 --> 00:09:09,020
Yeah, and you'll want to cover the whole back.

190
00:09:09,021 --> 00:09:11,020
I feel like Johnny culture seed over here.

191
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:15,380
Oh, you smell it.

192
00:09:15,381 --> 00:09:16,380
It's almost like bread.

193
00:09:16,381 --> 00:09:18,060
Like it has a yeast kind of note.

194
00:09:18,061 --> 00:09:20,180
Yeah, it does have a good smell to it.

195
00:09:20,181 --> 00:09:21,080
Wow, that's crazy.

196
00:09:21,081 --> 00:09:25,040
Yeah, like that sort of thing, like country hearth bread.

197
00:09:25,041 --> 00:09:27,580
That smell is the bacteria cultures

198
00:09:27,581 --> 00:09:30,080
breaking down the lactose sugar in the milk

199
00:09:30,081 --> 00:09:31,760
and starting to ferment.

200
00:09:31,761 --> 00:09:34,120
Most American mozzarella cheese makers

201
00:09:34,121 --> 00:09:38,360
cheat by adding an acid like vinegar or citric acid here.

202
00:09:38,361 --> 00:09:41,060
But at Caputo Brothers, they insist

203
00:09:41,061 --> 00:09:44,900
on making mozzarella the traditional Italian way

204
00:09:44,901 --> 00:09:47,340
using bacteria cultures.

205
00:09:47,341 --> 00:09:49,880
What do we do next with all this wonderful pasteurized milk?

206
00:09:49,881 --> 00:09:51,540
Next step is adding rennet.

207
00:09:51,541 --> 00:09:54,880
Rennet is an enzyme found initially in an animal's stomach.

208
00:09:54,881 --> 00:09:55,720
OK.

209
00:09:55,721 --> 00:09:58,360
So the rennet is what's going to set the milk.

210
00:09:58,361 --> 00:10:01,720
So it takes it from this liquid and kind of makes it like tofu.

211
00:10:01,721 --> 00:10:03,500
Wow. Let's go grab it.

212
00:10:03,501 --> 00:10:06,360
Without rennet, there would be no cheese.

213
00:10:06,361 --> 00:10:10,600
They found 3,000-year-old cheese in an Egyptian tomb,

214
00:10:10,601 --> 00:10:14,340
although nobody's dared to spread it on a cracker and take a bite.

215
00:10:14,341 --> 00:10:17,280
But cheese is even older than that.

216
00:10:17,281 --> 00:10:21,240
Before writing, before money, before architecture,

217
00:10:21,241 --> 00:10:22,580
there was cheese.

218
00:10:22,581 --> 00:10:26,840
Cheese probably dates to about 7,000 years ago.

219
00:10:26,841 --> 00:10:30,520
And it all started with our friend, Renit.

220
00:10:30,521 --> 00:10:33,560
The legend of how cheese was discovered,

221
00:10:33,561 --> 00:10:38,720
the story goes that a nomad had milk inside of an animal's

222
00:10:38,721 --> 00:10:42,240
stomach and was traveling with it from one place to another.

223
00:10:42,241 --> 00:10:44,080
And when he arrived to said destination,

224
00:10:44,081 --> 00:10:46,080
went to drink his milk, and out came cheese.

225
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:50,880
Rennet is an enzyme taken from the stomachs of slaughtered

226
00:10:50,980 --> 00:10:53,720
milk-drinking animals, mostly calves.

227
00:10:53,820 --> 00:10:56,520
When a cheese maker adds rennet to milk,

228
00:10:56,620 --> 00:10:59,620
the fats and proteins in the milk clump together

229
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:02,320
as solids called curds.

230
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:05,940
These curds separate from the liquid in the milk.

231
00:11:06,040 --> 00:11:10,140
All that leftover liquid is called whey.

232
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:12,340
The more whey that is pressed out of it,

233
00:11:12,440 --> 00:11:14,940
the harder the cheese gets.

234
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:15,840
Cheers to you.

235
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:16,640
Cheers.

236
00:11:16,641 --> 00:11:17,480
There you go.

237
00:11:17,481 --> 00:11:18,300
No, I'm just kidding.

238
00:11:18,301 --> 00:11:19,140
This is rennet.

239
00:11:19,141 --> 00:11:20,140
This would be disgusting to drink.

240
00:11:20,141 --> 00:11:22,680
OK, so you're going to take one of these spoons.

241
00:11:22,681 --> 00:11:26,380
So we're going to add the rennet on top of my spoon

242
00:11:26,381 --> 00:11:29,080
to distribute that better over the surface of the milk.

243
00:11:29,081 --> 00:11:30,180
Ooh, that's cool.

244
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:34,160
Great.

245
00:11:34,161 --> 00:11:35,700
So now you'll put your spoon in.

246
00:11:35,701 --> 00:11:37,600
It's like you're trying to bring the bottom of the milk

247
00:11:37,601 --> 00:11:39,460
to the top and the top to the bottom.

248
00:11:39,461 --> 00:11:41,160
So you're like folding it almost?

249
00:11:41,161 --> 00:11:42,340
Yeah.

250
00:11:42,341 --> 00:11:44,660
We're going to give the bacteria, cultures,

251
00:11:44,661 --> 00:11:48,660
and rennet some time to separate the curds from the whey.

252
00:11:48,760 --> 00:11:51,960
And a little bit later, we'll see what other delicious cheese

253
00:11:52,060 --> 00:11:54,740
they make with the leftover whey from this process.

254
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:02,380
At home in Brooklyn, New York, there's a saying.

255
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:04,880
What kind of bagel would you like with your schmear?

256
00:12:04,980 --> 00:12:07,320
And for those not familiar, a schmear

257
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:09,780
is usually a spread of cream cheese.

258
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:14,020
And the most famous of all cream cheese is Philadelphia,

259
00:12:14,021 --> 00:12:18,560
Of course, Americans eat the equivalent of the Empire State

260
00:12:18,561 --> 00:12:21,360
Building in cream cheese each year.

261
00:12:21,361 --> 00:12:23,360
It's soft, it's creamy.

262
00:12:23,361 --> 00:12:27,320
You could flavor it up and spread it on just about anything.

263
00:12:27,321 --> 00:12:31,220
It was named Philadelphia cream cheese in 1880.

264
00:12:31,221 --> 00:12:33,480
But if you go to the city of brotherly love

265
00:12:33,481 --> 00:12:36,060
and look for the Philly cream cheese factory,

266
00:12:36,061 --> 00:12:37,720
you won't find it.

267
00:12:37,721 --> 00:12:39,620
But guess where you will?

268
00:12:39,621 --> 00:12:41,280
In New York.

269
00:12:41,281 --> 00:12:43,360
It was called Philadelphia cream cheese

270
00:12:43,361 --> 00:12:47,820
because Philadelphia was considered the dairy area

271
00:12:47,821 --> 00:12:50,980
of the East Coast or maybe the whole country.

272
00:12:50,981 --> 00:12:53,120
And so it had this romance about it,

273
00:12:53,121 --> 00:12:54,220
but it was not made there.

274
00:12:54,221 --> 00:12:55,980
It was made in New York.

275
00:12:55,981 --> 00:12:57,340
Go figure.

276
00:12:57,341 --> 00:12:59,920
And it's not just bagels that get smeared

277
00:12:59,921 --> 00:13:01,820
with that cream cheese lovin'.

278
00:13:01,821 --> 00:13:05,360
This super spreadable is on or in the favorite

279
00:13:05,361 --> 00:13:07,160
of our most favorite foods,

280
00:13:07,161 --> 00:13:09,720
from stuffed French toast to sushi,

281
00:13:09,721 --> 00:13:12,060
from cheese balls to cheesecake.

282
00:13:12,061 --> 00:13:14,820
And can you even imagine carrot cake

283
00:13:14,821 --> 00:13:17,060
without cream cheese frosting?

284
00:13:17,061 --> 00:13:19,740
No way.

285
00:13:19,741 --> 00:13:23,560
But speaking of whey, we're heading back to Caputo Brothers

286
00:13:23,561 --> 00:13:27,200
to continue our firsthand look at how mozzarella is made

287
00:13:27,201 --> 00:13:29,360
in the traditional Italian style.

288
00:13:29,361 --> 00:13:31,820
We've added the bacteria cultures and rennet

289
00:13:31,821 --> 00:13:34,760
to separate it into curds and whey.

290
00:13:34,761 --> 00:13:38,180
So it's been about an hour since we added the rennet.

291
00:13:38,181 --> 00:13:40,600
Right, and you can see there's like this matte finish on top.

292
00:13:40,601 --> 00:13:43,800
Yep, so that's kind of the cream line coming to the top.

293
00:13:43,801 --> 00:13:45,400
But we're going to see if it's ready or not.

294
00:13:45,401 --> 00:13:48,100
And the way you do that is you stick your finger in here,

295
00:13:48,101 --> 00:13:49,200
and you're just going to...

296
00:13:49,201 --> 00:13:50,100
Oh, wow.

297
00:13:50,101 --> 00:13:51,900
Looks more like pudding now.

298
00:13:51,901 --> 00:13:53,400
It's got a skin on it.

299
00:13:53,401 --> 00:13:57,400
And if you can see that it nicely, smoothly cuts.

300
00:13:57,401 --> 00:13:58,300
See how that broke?

301
00:13:58,301 --> 00:13:59,200
Broke, yeah, like flan.

302
00:13:59,201 --> 00:14:00,000
Very nicely.

303
00:14:00,001 --> 00:14:01,500
Like flan.

304
00:14:01,501 --> 00:14:02,600
That means that it's ready.

305
00:14:02,601 --> 00:14:04,300
So now that we've established that we're at the right

306
00:14:04,301 --> 00:14:06,700
consistency, we've done our little poke test,

307
00:14:06,701 --> 00:14:07,500
what do we do next?

308
00:14:07,501 --> 00:14:08,400
We're about to cut it.

309
00:14:08,401 --> 00:14:09,300
Let's cut it.

310
00:14:09,301 --> 00:14:11,260
This is called a spino.

311
00:14:11,261 --> 00:14:13,640
This is what they use in Italy.

312
00:14:13,641 --> 00:14:17,640
We dive this in and bring it back to the surface.

313
00:14:17,641 --> 00:14:19,680
That's actually quite beautiful.

314
00:14:19,681 --> 00:14:21,120
Oh.

315
00:14:21,121 --> 00:14:22,580
So these are the curds.

316
00:14:22,581 --> 00:14:23,980
These are the curds.

317
00:14:23,981 --> 00:14:26,420
Mozzarella is a higher moisture cheese,

318
00:14:26,421 --> 00:14:30,120
so we're going to cut these to be a bit larger.

319
00:14:30,121 --> 00:14:32,080
Do you want to try cutting?

320
00:14:32,081 --> 00:14:36,000
Absolutely, just to be able to use the Gandalf staff over here.

321
00:14:36,001 --> 00:14:38,220
So you'll go down, and when you bring it up,

322
00:14:38,221 --> 00:14:40,080
turn it about 90 degrees.

323
00:14:40,081 --> 00:14:42,760
This to me is like the cheese equivalent

324
00:14:42,761 --> 00:14:45,220
of the way they would rake like a zen garden,

325
00:14:45,221 --> 00:14:47,020
except this is way more tasty.

326
00:14:47,021 --> 00:14:49,260
So how is my spino technique?

327
00:14:49,261 --> 00:14:50,800
You are doing a great job.

328
00:14:50,801 --> 00:14:52,540
I really do mean this in earnest.

329
00:14:52,541 --> 00:14:55,240
This is so relaxing.

330
00:14:55,241 --> 00:15:00,040
Oh no, mi spini, mi spini.

331
00:15:00,041 --> 00:15:01,340
Dude, forget Pagliacci.

332
00:15:01,341 --> 00:15:05,820
This is the worst tragedy in Italy right here.

333
00:15:05,821 --> 00:15:08,060
Now let's take a step back

334
00:15:08,061 --> 00:15:10,700
and take a look at another side of cheese.

335
00:15:10,701 --> 00:15:12,780
We all love cheese, right?

336
00:15:12,781 --> 00:15:14,840
But it might surprise you what some people

337
00:15:14,841 --> 00:15:17,660
are willing to pay or do to get it.

338
00:15:17,661 --> 00:15:21,920
Cheese is the number one stolen food in the world.

339
00:15:21,921 --> 00:15:25,560
In fact, 4% of all cheese made is stolen.

340
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:30,020
But some cheeses are more desired than others.

341
00:15:30,021 --> 00:15:31,700
Much like a fine painting,

342
00:15:31,701 --> 00:15:35,320
some cheeses take a great deal more effort to make than others,

343
00:15:35,321 --> 00:15:37,960
hence making them more valuable.

344
00:15:37,961 --> 00:15:40,360
I think that cheese is like fine art

345
00:15:40,361 --> 00:15:44,300
because it really holds a snapshot of time,

346
00:15:44,740 --> 00:15:48,280
of the environment, and the relation of animals to humans

347
00:15:48,281 --> 00:15:49,880
to the land that we live in.

348
00:15:50,340 --> 00:15:54,040
And when you eat that one piece of cheese, that's it.

349
00:15:54,180 --> 00:15:56,340
And you'll never be able to experience that again.

350
00:15:57,020 --> 00:15:59,780
Cheese is made from the milk of everything,

351
00:15:59,781 --> 00:16:02,080
from Holsteins to Himalayan yaks.

352
00:16:02,580 --> 00:16:04,980
One of the most expensive cheeses in the world

353
00:16:04,981 --> 00:16:07,440
is made from an unlikely source,

354
00:16:07,441 --> 00:16:09,680
high in the mountains of Serbia.

355
00:16:09,681 --> 00:16:11,480
So there's a donkey milk cheese

356
00:16:11,481 --> 00:16:14,520
that is the most expensive cheese that I've ever read about

357
00:16:14,521 --> 00:16:18,020
at $1,700 a pound, and it's called pool.

358
00:16:18,460 --> 00:16:20,240
I myself have not bought it.

359
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:22,940
The reason pool is so expensive

360
00:16:22,941 --> 00:16:26,480
is because it takes two weeks of milking these donkeys

361
00:16:26,481 --> 00:16:28,800
to make one pound of pool.

362
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:32,120
However, all this effort is rewarded

363
00:16:32,121 --> 00:16:35,120
by a cheese that has one-third as much fat

364
00:16:35,121 --> 00:16:38,820
and has 60 times more vitamin C than most cheese.

365
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:42,820
Those rich enough to try it say pool tastes like manchego

366
00:16:42,821 --> 00:16:45,260
with a deeper, richer flavor.

367
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:50,600
And so this on top here, this liquid, this is the whey?

368
00:16:50,840 --> 00:16:51,940
This is all whey.

369
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:54,680
We'll let this sit about 10 minutes.

370
00:16:54,820 --> 00:16:56,740
The curds will knit together,

371
00:16:56,940 --> 00:16:59,260
and the whey will continue to come to the surface.

372
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:02,320
And then we'll take the whey off and make crocotte.

373
00:17:02,321 --> 00:17:05,320
At Caputo Brothers, they actually use their way

374
00:17:05,321 --> 00:17:08,820
to make an amazing ricotta, which we'll get to later.

375
00:17:08,821 --> 00:17:14,160
But Elizabeth is going to let me taste some of the fresh curds.

376
00:17:14,161 --> 00:17:17,940
So it has, it's literally, it moves like silken tofu

377
00:17:17,941 --> 00:17:20,080
or the albumin of an egg.

378
00:17:20,081 --> 00:17:21,200
So I love this.

379
00:17:21,201 --> 00:17:24,080
I'm gonna take a step back for health and safety reasons.

380
00:17:24,081 --> 00:17:32,780
So it has the consistency like a silken tofu,

381
00:17:32,781 --> 00:17:36,720
but because it's milk, it's more substantial.

382
00:17:37,160 --> 00:17:40,120
I don't know, is it possible to taste more milky than milk?

383
00:17:40,720 --> 00:17:42,240
Well, that makes sense.

384
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:44,660
Cheese is just concentrated milk.

385
00:17:44,900 --> 00:17:47,640
We've taken off all of this whey, which is the extra liquid,

386
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,200
so you have concentrated milk flavor in your mouth.

387
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:51,680
Oh, because all the moisture's gone.

388
00:17:51,800 --> 00:17:52,500
Exactly.

389
00:17:52,540 --> 00:17:53,440
It's quite crazy.

390
00:17:53,441 --> 00:17:56,240
It liquefies the moment it touches your tongue.

391
00:17:56,241 --> 00:17:59,240
It has a decadence and a dessert-like quality,

392
00:17:59,241 --> 00:18:02,780
devoid of sugar, devoid of vanilla, devoid of cream,

393
00:18:02,781 --> 00:18:06,480
and all it is is just the rennet, skill, time,

394
00:18:06,481 --> 00:18:08,160
and really good quality milk.

395
00:18:08,161 --> 00:18:11,120
And now it's going to become something fascinating

396
00:18:11,121 --> 00:18:15,000
and delicious, part tradition and part modern marvel.

397
00:18:15,001 --> 00:18:17,400
But this is more a melting modern marvel,

398
00:18:17,401 --> 00:18:20,260
mouth-watering melting modern marvel at that.

399
00:18:20,261 --> 00:18:22,400
I'm very excited, and I'll be using more

400
00:18:22,401 --> 00:18:25,400
alliterative sentences as this episode continues.

401
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:29,500
So let's chug along with this cheery cheese making, children.

402
00:18:33,440 --> 00:18:38,240
The Great Pyramid of Giza weighs 13 billion pounds.

403
00:18:38,340 --> 00:18:41,560
That's how much cheese Americans eat each year.

404
00:18:41,660 --> 00:18:44,260
And artisan cheese makers like Caputo Brothers

405
00:18:44,360 --> 00:18:47,100
can't come close to meeting that demand.

406
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:49,600
So we're going to a Kroger supermarket's

407
00:18:49,601 --> 00:18:53,100
Cheese Conversion Facility in Crawfordsville, Indiana

408
00:18:53,101 --> 00:18:55,320
to check out some of the technology

409
00:18:55,321 --> 00:18:59,940
that helps get all that cheese from the farm to your fridge.

410
00:19:00,360 --> 00:19:03,040
It reminds me of the last Remainters of the Lost Ark

411
00:19:03,041 --> 00:19:05,320
when you're going to the shot of the warehouse

412
00:19:05,321 --> 00:19:07,040
and just walking back through there.

413
00:19:07,041 --> 00:19:10,160
Just rows and rows of cheese.

414
00:19:12,700 --> 00:19:16,000
This facility supplies Kroger Supermarkets

415
00:19:16,001 --> 00:19:19,200
with literally tons of cheese.

416
00:19:19,201 --> 00:19:23,680
in 2,700 stores across 35 states.

417
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:25,860
Kroger doesn't make the cheese.

418
00:19:26,180 --> 00:19:31,120
Instead, they get truckloads full of 640-pound blocks,

419
00:19:31,280 --> 00:19:34,960
and their job here is to feed it into all sorts of machines

420
00:19:34,961 --> 00:19:38,220
to convert these massive blocks into cheese slices,

421
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:40,940
shreds, and smaller blocks.

422
00:19:41,300 --> 00:19:46,520
We usually bring in about 500,000 pounds of cheese a day,

423
00:19:46,521 --> 00:19:51,260
typically between 2.5 to 3 million pounds of cheese a week.

424
00:19:52,100 --> 00:19:57,340
In each one of these boxes is 640 pounds of cheese.

425
00:19:57,720 --> 00:19:59,420
Within two to three weeks,

426
00:19:59,480 --> 00:20:01,380
all this cheese will be used up completely.

427
00:20:05,160 --> 00:20:10,560
The facility processes 120 million pounds of cheese each year

428
00:20:10,561 --> 00:20:15,860
to meet this demand requires milk from 60,000 cows.

429
00:20:15,861 --> 00:20:18,900
That's a train of cows that could reach the summit

430
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:22,900
of Mount Everest, not once, but 15 times.

431
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:27,000
Converting these 640-pound blocks into slices and shreds

432
00:20:27,100 --> 00:20:29,100
is no easy task.

433
00:20:29,200 --> 00:20:31,200
And production lead Matthew Howard

434
00:20:31,300 --> 00:20:34,420
is going to show us how they do it.

435
00:20:34,520 --> 00:20:38,320
So let's begin at the start of the actual production process.

436
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:42,420
This machine takes our giant 640-pound blocks of cheese,

437
00:20:42,520 --> 00:20:45,560
lifts it into position, and it goes down the conveyor belt

438
00:20:45,561 --> 00:20:48,060
over there to our wire guillotine.

439
00:20:48,061 --> 00:20:51,060
These big 640s, as they're known,

440
00:20:51,061 --> 00:20:54,360
get sliced into slabs, then move down the belt

441
00:20:54,361 --> 00:20:57,800
to the ultrasonic knife.

442
00:20:57,801 --> 00:21:01,100
This device that sounds like it's right out of a spy movie

443
00:21:01,101 --> 00:21:05,520
uses a series of blades that emit high-frequency sound waves

444
00:21:05,521 --> 00:21:08,520
to slice through these huge blocks of cheese.

445
00:21:08,521 --> 00:21:10,720
The sound waves help keep the blades

446
00:21:10,721 --> 00:21:14,020
from sticking to the cheese, and then they go into

447
00:21:14,021 --> 00:21:18,020
into a density scanner, which takes an x-ray of the log

448
00:21:18,120 --> 00:21:21,560
to help the slicer know exactly how to cut it.

449
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:26,060
This machine cuts more than 3 million cheese slices a day,

450
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:30,260
seven days a week, 365 days a year.

451
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:33,080
And traveling at thousands of revolutions a minute,

452
00:21:33,180 --> 00:21:35,380
this blade not only slices cheese,

453
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:38,920
but also inserts paper in order to separate

454
00:21:39,020 --> 00:21:40,780
each individual slice.

455
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:43,380
This all happens so fast, you have to use

456
00:21:43,381 --> 00:21:45,920
to use an ultra-slow-motion camera

457
00:21:45,921 --> 00:21:49,180
to see this magic in action.

458
00:21:49,181 --> 00:21:52,180
We actually produce 380,000 cases a day.

459
00:21:52,181 --> 00:21:55,220
All the cheese for Kroger in America.

460
00:21:55,221 --> 00:21:57,860
All this cheese is getting loaded onto as many

461
00:21:57,861 --> 00:22:01,200
as 40 trucks a day and sent out to grocery stores

462
00:22:01,201 --> 00:22:05,200
across America to make sure we get the cheese we need.

463
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:09,840
Now let's get back to making the cheese.

464
00:22:09,841 --> 00:22:12,740
At this point in the process, most cheese makers drain

465
00:22:12,741 --> 00:22:15,140
strain the whey and get rid of it as a byproduct.

466
00:22:15,141 --> 00:22:19,680
But here at Caputo Brothers, they use their whey to make ricotta.

467
00:22:19,681 --> 00:22:21,840
All right, it's been about 10 minutes.

468
00:22:21,841 --> 00:22:26,920
So as the song says, I want you to show me the whey.

469
00:22:26,921 --> 00:22:28,360
We'll know if it's ready to whey off.

470
00:22:28,361 --> 00:22:30,960
If you stick your hands in and you don't feel those curds

471
00:22:30,961 --> 00:22:33,900
right at the top, then we know we can take that top

472
00:22:33,901 --> 00:22:34,900
and put it away.

473
00:22:34,901 --> 00:22:35,900
Yeah, I don't feel them at all.

474
00:22:35,901 --> 00:22:36,900
So how far? They've sunken?

475
00:22:36,901 --> 00:22:38,300
They've sunken quite a bit.

476
00:22:38,301 --> 00:22:40,160
So we'll take some whey off now.

477
00:22:40,161 --> 00:22:42,240
And we'll use these strainers and pitchers.

478
00:22:42,241 --> 00:22:44,900
And strain it through in case there's any curds.

479
00:22:44,901 --> 00:22:46,080
Is that good?

480
00:22:46,081 --> 00:22:46,980
Perfect.

481
00:22:46,981 --> 00:22:51,080
And then you'll just put it through your strainer.

482
00:22:51,081 --> 00:22:52,580
And again, this is tradition?

483
00:22:52,581 --> 00:22:53,580
This is what we're doing?

484
00:22:53,581 --> 00:22:54,580
This is how they do it in Italy.

485
00:22:54,581 --> 00:22:58,280
They take the whey, and then they'll re-cook it.

486
00:22:58,281 --> 00:23:00,700
Oh, re-cooking it, which is?

487
00:23:00,701 --> 00:23:01,700
Ricotta.

488
00:23:01,701 --> 00:23:02,700
Ricotta.

489
00:23:02,701 --> 00:23:03,700
I love that.

490
00:23:03,701 --> 00:23:09,040
I have to say, I was today years old when I found out that ricotta and mozzarella can

491
00:23:09,041 --> 00:23:11,000
both be made from the same batch of milk.

492
00:23:11,001 --> 00:23:14,000
So I'm kind of amazed at what kind of alchemy we're using

493
00:23:14,001 --> 00:23:16,580
to turn this yellow liquid

494
00:23:16,581 --> 00:23:19,440
into that creamy, grainy, wonderful cheese.

495
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,600
The alchemy used is both simple,

496
00:23:22,740 --> 00:23:24,580
like cheese making in general is,

497
00:23:24,740 --> 00:23:26,680
and ingeniously modern,

498
00:23:27,040 --> 00:23:29,580
as steam is used to heat up the whey.

499
00:23:30,020 --> 00:23:32,700
But here at Caputo, a steam wand

500
00:23:32,701 --> 00:23:35,240
helps the process work its magic.

501
00:23:35,940 --> 00:23:36,920
This way, right?

502
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:38,140
Uh, it goes down.

503
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:39,640
See?

504
00:23:39,641 --> 00:23:41,280
You sure you want me to do this?

505
00:23:41,281 --> 00:23:42,780
Because I have a feeling this is going to go

506
00:23:42,781 --> 00:23:43,780
blow my damn eyebrows off.

507
00:23:43,781 --> 00:23:45,280
It's going to be really loud.

508
00:23:45,281 --> 00:23:46,280
All right.

509
00:23:46,281 --> 00:23:49,780
And with that little lever, I begin the crescendoing sound

510
00:23:49,781 --> 00:23:53,280
of a mini rocket blasting off.

511
00:23:53,281 --> 00:23:56,280
It's a steam wand heating the way and beginning the cooking

512
00:23:56,281 --> 00:23:58,780
in the ricotta process.

513
00:23:58,781 --> 00:24:01,780
I'm amazed at this method of making ricotta,

514
00:24:01,781 --> 00:24:04,280
and I have to see how this turns out.

515
00:24:04,281 --> 00:24:06,780
out.

516
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:09,680
The story goes that 800 years ago,

517
00:24:09,780 --> 00:24:12,780
a Sicilian cheese maker spilled some whey into a pot

518
00:24:12,880 --> 00:24:18,120
of boiling water, and mamma mia, ricotta was made.

519
00:24:18,220 --> 00:24:20,320
Here at Caputo Brothers Creamery,

520
00:24:20,420 --> 00:24:23,240
our whey has been sitting for about 40 minutes

521
00:24:23,340 --> 00:24:24,640
in the steam bath.

522
00:24:24,740 --> 00:24:27,740
What was the whey byproduct of a different cheese

523
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:31,640
has turned into a frothy, beautiful ricotta.

524
00:24:31,740 --> 00:24:34,280
If you want to taste it, now is the best time.

525
00:24:34,281 --> 00:24:35,180
Is it?

526
00:24:35,181 --> 00:24:37,120
When it's warm, oh, it's delicious.

527
00:24:37,121 --> 00:24:38,820
You're just going to take a light scoop.

528
00:24:38,821 --> 00:24:40,620
You don't really want to dive down.

529
00:24:40,621 --> 00:24:42,920
You just want to take a little shave off the top.

530
00:24:42,921 --> 00:24:45,420
OK.

531
00:24:45,421 --> 00:24:47,720
Just like that.

532
00:24:47,721 --> 00:24:48,920
This is very new.

533
00:24:48,921 --> 00:24:49,920
This is crazy.

534
00:24:49,921 --> 00:24:53,340
Now it's going to be really hot.

535
00:24:53,341 --> 00:24:55,340
Ooh, I love that smell so much.

536
00:25:00,840 --> 00:25:03,840
That's gorgeous.

537
00:25:03,841 --> 00:25:05,880
Oh, man.

538
00:25:05,881 --> 00:25:07,680
It's, like, velvety.

539
00:25:07,681 --> 00:25:10,940
I've never had ricotta-like custard before.

540
00:25:10,941 --> 00:25:12,840
You've ruined me now for ricotta.

541
00:25:12,841 --> 00:25:14,020
Nice job, Caputo.

542
00:25:14,021 --> 00:25:17,020
Now I can't eat any other cheese ever.

543
00:25:17,021 --> 00:25:18,820
The only thing that comes close

544
00:25:18,821 --> 00:25:21,700
to eating warm cheese fresh from the source

545
00:25:21,701 --> 00:25:25,320
is eating warm cheese on pasta.

546
00:25:25,321 --> 00:25:27,700
And our favorite pasta-cheese combo,

547
00:25:27,701 --> 00:25:30,860
that's right, macaroni and cheese.

548
00:25:30,861 --> 00:25:33,440
The first recorded mac and cheese recipe

549
00:25:33,441 --> 00:25:35,940
goes back 800 years,

550
00:25:35,941 --> 00:25:38,740
but it became one of America's best-selling brands

551
00:25:38,741 --> 00:25:42,680
in 1937, when Kraft macaroni and cheese

552
00:25:42,681 --> 00:25:46,080
was developed as an affordable meal during the Depression.

553
00:25:46,081 --> 00:25:49,820
Today, Kraft sells more than a million boxes

554
00:25:49,821 --> 00:25:52,760
of mac and cheese every day.

555
00:25:52,761 --> 00:25:55,800
And, believe it or not, it's the unofficial food

556
00:25:55,801 --> 00:25:58,100
of our Canadian neighbors to the north.

557
00:25:58,101 --> 00:26:02,840
The Canucks eat 55% more Kraft dinner, as they call it,

558
00:26:02,841 --> 00:26:05,840
than we do, and they eat it with ketchup on top.

559
00:26:09,340 --> 00:26:13,180
I got to taste warm ricotta fresh out of the vat,

560
00:26:13,181 --> 00:26:17,080
but that was only the infant stage of Caputo's ricotta.

561
00:26:17,081 --> 00:26:20,920
I got very excited when Rin Caputo pulled me aside

562
00:26:20,921 --> 00:26:23,000
to taste the finished product.

563
00:26:23,001 --> 00:26:26,960
I heard that you absolutely loved eating the ricotta,

564
00:26:26,961 --> 00:26:28,960
but most people don't have access to that,

565
00:26:28,961 --> 00:26:32,840
so I wanted you to have it once it's had a chance to have it.

566
00:26:32,841 --> 00:26:34,680
And notice it's much firmer.

567
00:26:34,681 --> 00:26:35,680
Look at it.

568
00:26:35,681 --> 00:26:36,680
The cheesecake.

569
00:26:36,681 --> 00:26:37,680
It's so delicate.

570
00:26:37,681 --> 00:26:40,680
And it's really creamy.

571
00:26:40,681 --> 00:26:42,180
That's ridiculous.

572
00:26:42,181 --> 00:26:46,180
Borders on cream cheese or, like,

573
00:26:46,181 --> 00:26:48,680
mascarpone in terms of its smoothness.

574
00:26:48,681 --> 00:26:52,600
That in a lasagna is such a game changer.

575
00:26:52,601 --> 00:26:55,000
Unlike any ricotta I've had, it's superb.

576
00:26:55,001 --> 00:26:57,500
That ricotta was amazing.

577
00:26:57,501 --> 00:27:00,500
But there is this other cheese next to it

578
00:27:00,501 --> 00:27:03,000
that looks like it took a roll in the dirt.

579
00:27:03,001 --> 00:27:05,000
You know I got to try that one, too.

580
00:27:05,001 --> 00:27:08,000
So this is our ricotta salata.

581
00:27:08,001 --> 00:27:10,340
Instead of putting it in the baskets,

582
00:27:10,341 --> 00:27:12,040
we press it and we age it.

583
00:27:12,041 --> 00:27:14,040
How long do you think we age it for?

584
00:27:14,041 --> 00:27:16,880
Oh, you never guess a lady's cheese age.

585
00:27:16,881 --> 00:27:17,960
So I'm going to guess.

586
00:27:17,961 --> 00:27:19,380
I know nothing about cheese making.

587
00:27:19,381 --> 00:27:21,260
I'm going to say six months.

588
00:27:21,261 --> 00:27:22,260
Good guess.

589
00:27:22,261 --> 00:27:25,160
So in six weeks, we get this cheese to look like this.

590
00:27:25,161 --> 00:27:27,460
So how in the hell was six months a good guess?

591
00:27:27,461 --> 00:27:30,500
Why are you playing with me like this, Ren?

592
00:27:30,501 --> 00:27:33,000
Because the number six was in it?

593
00:27:33,001 --> 00:27:33,701
Look at it.

594
00:27:33,702 --> 00:27:35,800
It cuts like a birthday cake.

595
00:27:35,801 --> 00:27:38,800
OK, so I'm going to cut a wedge out here.

596
00:27:38,801 --> 00:27:40,500
Now, wait for it.

597
00:27:40,501 --> 00:27:41,700
Ah!

598
00:27:41,701 --> 00:27:43,900
That is so awesome.

599
00:27:43,901 --> 00:27:45,400
And it's the same cheese.

600
00:27:45,401 --> 00:27:46,200
It is.

601
00:27:46,201 --> 00:27:47,100
Look at that.

602
00:27:47,101 --> 00:27:48,900
You can see it is absolutely solid.

603
00:27:48,901 --> 00:27:50,100
It's breathtaking.

604
00:27:50,101 --> 00:27:53,900
So this is a cheese that traditionally is quite salty.

605
00:27:53,901 --> 00:27:55,700
So you have to slice it very thin,

606
00:27:55,701 --> 00:27:57,900
which is why I'm slicing it on a wire.

607
00:27:57,901 --> 00:27:59,000
It's like a cheese crumb.

608
00:27:59,001 --> 00:28:02,000
Two ricotta come into a bar, one was assaulted.

609
00:28:02,001 --> 00:28:03,640
I love it.

610
00:28:03,641 --> 00:28:07,340
So go ahead and try that and see what you think.

611
00:28:07,341 --> 00:28:09,640
Oh, that's so good.

612
00:28:09,641 --> 00:28:10,520
And there's a...

613
00:28:10,521 --> 00:28:11,640
A mushroom kind of earthiness?

614
00:28:11,641 --> 00:28:12,640
A funky.

615
00:28:12,641 --> 00:28:15,480
I was about to say, like a funky, it's like, yeah,

616
00:28:15,481 --> 00:28:19,420
like an earthy, salty creaminess.

617
00:28:19,421 --> 00:28:20,121
I love it.

618
00:28:20,122 --> 00:28:22,420
I really love it.

619
00:28:22,421 --> 00:28:25,300
This finished Caputo cheese may have ruined me

620
00:28:25,301 --> 00:28:26,920
for any other ricotta.

621
00:28:26,921 --> 00:28:30,520
And I got a feeling that this fresh mozzarella I got coming up

622
00:28:30,521 --> 00:28:32,460
is going to be even better.

623
00:28:32,461 --> 00:28:33,920
Practice safe lunch.

624
00:28:33,921 --> 00:28:34,920
Use a condiment.

625
00:28:38,300 --> 00:28:41,760
All right, we've drained the whey and made ricotta from it.

626
00:28:41,761 --> 00:28:44,440
And what was left behind were the curds.

627
00:28:44,441 --> 00:28:48,200
Now it's time to turn those curds into cheese.

628
00:28:48,201 --> 00:28:53,320
It's time for the mozzarella magic to start to take shape.

629
00:28:53,321 --> 00:28:55,740
All right, so hand me a block and you can take one.

630
00:28:55,741 --> 00:28:56,640
OK.

631
00:28:56,641 --> 00:28:57,680
Ooh, it feels cool.

632
00:28:57,681 --> 00:28:58,580
Yeah.

633
00:28:58,581 --> 00:28:59,680
OK, so what do I do?

634
00:28:59,681 --> 00:29:03,380
So you're just going to try to break it up into smaller chunks.

635
00:29:03,381 --> 00:29:04,280
OK.

636
00:29:04,281 --> 00:29:07,380
Maybe somewhere around the size of a quarter.

637
00:29:07,381 --> 00:29:08,680
Not too small?

638
00:29:08,681 --> 00:29:09,780
Nope, that's perfect.

639
00:29:09,781 --> 00:29:11,480
Doing what we're doing now, stretching by hand,

640
00:29:11,481 --> 00:29:12,800
this is what makes it artisan.

641
00:29:18,200 --> 00:29:20,700
So the next step is we're going to add a little bit of salt.

642
00:29:20,701 --> 00:29:24,140
So this, we kind of just do by hand.

643
00:29:24,141 --> 00:29:25,140
OK.

644
00:29:25,141 --> 00:29:29,280
I usually find two medium-sized fistfuls is about enough.

645
00:29:29,281 --> 00:29:32,180
We start by tempering the curds with a shot

646
00:29:32,181 --> 00:29:35,180
of 190-degree water.

647
00:29:35,181 --> 00:29:38,700
Then we stir the curds using plastic wands

648
00:29:38,701 --> 00:29:40,780
that Elizabeth and her team lovingly

649
00:29:40,781 --> 00:29:43,500
call their magic sticks.

650
00:29:43,501 --> 00:29:47,260
You can already see the mozzarella forming.

651
00:29:47,261 --> 00:29:50,040
OK, I'm just going to need some nachos and some privacy, guys.

652
00:29:50,041 --> 00:29:52,680
You guys all got to go home.

653
00:29:52,681 --> 00:29:55,280
For me, and probably most Americans,

654
00:29:55,281 --> 00:29:59,020
mozzarella lives at the heart of Comfort Food Central.

655
00:29:59,021 --> 00:30:02,280
Can you believe each of us eats, on average,

656
00:30:02,281 --> 00:30:04,920
about 13 pounds of it a year?

657
00:30:04,921 --> 00:30:08,560
But most of us are introduced to America's favorite cheese

658
00:30:08,561 --> 00:30:12,560
as kids by peeling those long strips of string cheese

659
00:30:12,561 --> 00:30:14,140
and stuffing them in your mouth.

660
00:30:15,400 --> 00:30:17,340
The string cheese craze in America

661
00:30:17,341 --> 00:30:20,240
is credited by many to Francis Baker,

662
00:30:20,241 --> 00:30:23,000
a mozzarella cheese maker in St. Cloud, Wisconsin,

663
00:30:23,001 --> 00:30:26,540
who, in 1977, had the brilliant idea

664
00:30:26,541 --> 00:30:30,040
to package individual pieces of mozzarella.

665
00:30:30,041 --> 00:30:32,080
But he needed a hook.

666
00:30:32,081 --> 00:30:34,140
The first versions of string cheese

667
00:30:34,141 --> 00:30:36,300
were twisted ropes of mozzarella

668
00:30:36,301 --> 00:30:40,280
that he tried to sell to bars of all places.

669
00:30:40,281 --> 00:30:44,840
Soon, they rebranded as the perfect healthy kid snack

670
00:30:44,841 --> 00:30:48,600
and repackaged so they were easier for little hands to hold it

671
00:30:48,601 --> 00:30:52,300
and became the favorite snack food for school lunches

672
00:30:52,301 --> 00:30:54,280
across America.

673
00:30:54,281 --> 00:30:58,720
Soon, other bands like Frigo Cheeseheads, Sargento,

674
00:30:58,721 --> 00:31:03,640
Palio, and Kraft made string cheese into a big business.

675
00:31:03,641 --> 00:31:05,720
You can eat it any way you want.

676
00:31:05,721 --> 00:31:06,980
It's a free country.

677
00:31:06,981 --> 00:31:09,660
But it's called string cheese for a reason.

678
00:31:13,120 --> 00:31:14,520
And now we're going to stretch it.

679
00:31:14,521 --> 00:31:17,560
Pick it up by the middle on your magic stick,

680
00:31:17,561 --> 00:31:19,020
and you'll be able to stretch it.

681
00:31:21,660 --> 00:31:23,740
Tell me Caputo Brothers is not making magic.

682
00:31:23,741 --> 00:31:25,940
This is why it's called the magic stick.

683
00:31:25,941 --> 00:31:29,240
Stretching cheese, or as the Italians call it,

684
00:31:29,241 --> 00:31:32,800
pasta filata, aligns the proteins in the cheese

685
00:31:32,801 --> 00:31:35,820
and gives the mozzarella that gooey stretchiness

686
00:31:35,821 --> 00:31:38,380
that you want when you're pulling a slice of pizza

687
00:31:38,381 --> 00:31:39,580
from the pie.

688
00:31:39,581 --> 00:31:41,520
It's what makes string cheese stringy

689
00:31:41,521 --> 00:31:45,160
and gives mozzarella that gorgeous mouthfeel that it has.

690
00:31:45,161 --> 00:31:47,540
if this were like fried right here,

691
00:31:47,541 --> 00:31:50,100
and I just fill one of these with marinara.

692
00:31:50,101 --> 00:31:51,820
I just want to double dutch with this

693
00:31:51,821 --> 00:31:53,040
for the rest of my life.

694
00:31:55,900 --> 00:31:59,460
Cheese isn't always decadent and delicious.

695
00:31:59,461 --> 00:32:02,880
In the 1970s, government cheese was born.

696
00:32:02,881 --> 00:32:05,100
And even though it was super sketchy,

697
00:32:05,101 --> 00:32:08,020
it became part of most Americans' lives,

698
00:32:08,021 --> 00:32:09,580
and we learned to love it.

699
00:32:10,940 --> 00:32:14,120
In the 1970s, prices for milk went way up,

700
00:32:14,121 --> 00:32:16,040
and then they came crashing way down.

701
00:32:16,041 --> 00:32:18,500
It was really hard times for dairy farmers.

702
00:32:18,501 --> 00:32:21,380
So under US President Jimmy Carter,

703
00:32:21,381 --> 00:32:25,100
the US government pumped $2 billion into the industry.

704
00:32:25,101 --> 00:32:28,860
And they used that cash to produce more and more milk.

705
00:32:28,861 --> 00:32:32,360
Except now, the dairy farmers had a different problem.

706
00:32:32,361 --> 00:32:35,420
They had way more milk than they could sell.

707
00:32:35,421 --> 00:32:38,060
So the US government stepped in again.

708
00:32:38,061 --> 00:32:41,620
They decided to buy up all the surplus milk,

709
00:32:41,621 --> 00:32:46,060
And they used it to make, you guessed it, processed cheese.

710
00:32:46,061 --> 00:32:49,540
And that actually caused another problem.

711
00:32:49,541 --> 00:32:52,340
Cheese has to be stored under temperature-controlled

712
00:32:52,341 --> 00:32:54,740
facilities, and that was really expensive.

713
00:32:54,741 --> 00:32:57,500
So the US government started distributing the cheese

714
00:32:57,501 --> 00:32:58,740
to people who needed it.

715
00:32:58,741 --> 00:33:01,900
This was called government cheese.

716
00:33:01,901 --> 00:33:04,920
This vast surplus of government cheese

717
00:33:04,921 --> 00:33:08,380
was processed cheese made from a blend of different cheese

718
00:33:08,381 --> 00:33:10,220
products and emulsifiers.

719
00:33:10,221 --> 00:33:13,460
It came in gigantic five-pound blocks.

720
00:33:13,461 --> 00:33:15,840
Often, it was covered in mold from sitting

721
00:33:15,841 --> 00:33:19,920
in huge underground storage facilities for up to 10 years.

722
00:33:19,921 --> 00:33:21,380
And the taste?

723
00:33:21,381 --> 00:33:23,600
The nicest thing the government could say about it

724
00:33:23,601 --> 00:33:27,560
was that it slices and melts well.

725
00:33:27,561 --> 00:33:31,380
Now, government cheese has a bit of a mixed reputation.

726
00:33:31,381 --> 00:33:34,040
On the one hand, it's associated with hard times.

727
00:33:34,041 --> 00:33:37,100
But on the other hand, a lot of people grew up eating it.

728
00:33:37,101 --> 00:33:40,480
And so it's this nostalgic taste of childhood for them.

729
00:33:41,160 --> 00:33:44,280
Mark Wahlberg and his siblings grew up eating government cheese,

730
00:33:44,281 --> 00:33:47,280
and they actually opened a burger chain called Wahlburgers

731
00:33:47,840 --> 00:33:50,120
that offered government cheese for free,

732
00:33:50,220 --> 00:33:51,820
as a free topping on all of their burgers.

733
00:33:54,640 --> 00:33:57,440
To see the milk arrive there,

734
00:33:57,980 --> 00:34:00,460
like that's the back of the tank that we saw

735
00:34:00,461 --> 00:34:03,760
when Sean delivered the milk in the beginning of the day.

736
00:34:04,140 --> 00:34:05,560
We saw it pasteurized,

737
00:34:05,561 --> 00:34:11,000
and to see it kind of go milk to curd to ribbons of cheesy

738
00:34:11,001 --> 00:34:12,100
goodness.

739
00:34:12,101 --> 00:34:14,900
Seriously, this is gorgeous.

740
00:34:14,901 --> 00:34:17,100
Oh, wow.

741
00:34:17,101 --> 00:34:20,000
Well, once it's stretched, grab a clump.

742
00:34:20,001 --> 00:34:21,000
OK.

743
00:34:21,001 --> 00:34:24,020
And you're trying to work that outer layer of cheese

744
00:34:24,021 --> 00:34:25,620
very thin.

745
00:34:25,621 --> 00:34:28,820
So you're kind of smoothing it, smoothing it.

746
00:34:28,821 --> 00:34:31,460
And all the while, you'll collect it with your other hand.

747
00:34:31,461 --> 00:34:32,960
There you go.

748
00:34:32,961 --> 00:34:35,460
Now you're going to try to cut off the circulation

749
00:34:35,461 --> 00:34:37,460
with that finger as you grab it.

750
00:34:37,461 --> 00:34:39,460
And you're just going to pinch, pinch, pinch, pinch,

751
00:34:39,461 --> 00:34:41,460
pinch until you can feel it break.

752
00:34:41,461 --> 00:34:42,460
Uh-huh.

753
00:34:42,461 --> 00:34:43,460
I'm going to put this in here.

754
00:34:43,461 --> 00:34:44,460
Yep.

755
00:34:44,461 --> 00:34:47,460
This is my favorite time to eat this cheese.

756
00:34:47,461 --> 00:34:48,460
Really?

757
00:34:48,461 --> 00:34:49,460
Warm, freshly stretched.

758
00:34:49,461 --> 00:34:52,460
Yeah, it's just so gorgeous.

759
00:34:55,460 --> 00:34:56,460
Is it OK if I try a little?

760
00:34:56,461 --> 00:34:57,460
Absolutely.

761
00:34:57,461 --> 00:34:58,460
How could I not do that?

762
00:34:58,461 --> 00:34:59,760
Is it OK if I try a little?

763
00:34:59,761 --> 00:35:00,760
Absolutely.

764
00:35:00,761 --> 00:35:03,300
How could I not want to try this?

765
00:35:03,301 --> 00:35:06,300
It just, it's warm and it's silky when you touch it.

766
00:35:09,100 --> 00:35:10,100
Whoo!

767
00:35:21,220 --> 00:35:23,020
So good.

768
00:35:23,021 --> 00:35:25,580
This has to be like in the Caputo benefits package,

769
00:35:25,581 --> 00:35:30,160
like vision, dental, and the ability to eat top-quality mozzarella,

770
00:35:30,161 --> 00:35:33,580
warm and stringy out of the stretching process.

771
00:35:33,581 --> 00:35:36,420
The milk is the first note you get.

772
00:35:40,420 --> 00:35:43,460
All I can say is I love my job.

773
00:35:43,461 --> 00:35:47,700
Just like a well-oiled baseball glove,

774
00:35:47,701 --> 00:35:50,040
a bottle of wine, or Tom Hanks,

775
00:35:50,041 --> 00:35:53,380
cheese is something that gets better with age,

776
00:35:53,381 --> 00:35:58,120
thanks to microscopic organisms in dank, dark places.

777
00:35:58,220 --> 00:36:00,880
We've been granted access to a cave in a place

